    "q-111897": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111897",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{y}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
