    "q-143885": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143885",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[Y\\sqrt{X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2{y}^{3}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\sqrt{X}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}y\\sqrt{x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4[-\\frac{1}{y}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
