    "q-143881": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143881",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y+1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot (y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{3}+{y}^{2}){\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{3}+{y}^{2}){[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{8}{3}({y}^{3}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
