    "q-143875": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143875",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[Y\\operatorname{cos}⁡X],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\operatorname{cos}⁡X] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
