    "q-143905": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143905",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{{e}^{X-Y}}{1-X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14154787/assets/bc44d4c99cf60148685a99b5c3b5ea2e732ea4b9939aefe5ba465d46e92c5ea8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{{e}^{X-Y}}{1-X}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-x}\\cdot [y]_{y=x}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-x}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{e}^{x}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
