    "q-143831": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143831",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{Y}{7X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}y,\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{y}{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14154787/assets/54777bc840acd1d1c24da08ccb99025b039af9c47421602a3d9dd73df2a91779.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{Y}{7X}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{y}{7x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{7x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\frac{y}{7x}\\cdot \\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x-{x}^{7})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}]}_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8})-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
