    "q-143855": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143855",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X-Y)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le x; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},$ where $f(x)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14154787/assets/ff35a5c562607b4ac314d3ae755156d75baf5d8b01b813f6b59d67dac540e154.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X-Y)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)]_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}(1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{{\\pi }^{2}}(2-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-143855-a",
